// lib/core/string.kite — the canonical CORE home for String (kite::core::string): the boxed `class String`
// representation + its non-allocating queries, the `+`/`==`/`<`/hash operator overloads, the
// `Int.toStr`/interpolation-`show` path, and the ALLOCATING transforms (substr/repeat/join). The growable
// `StrBuilder` byte buffer lives in kite::core::strbuilder; the opt-in UTF-8 tier (the Int-tier
// utf8SeqLen/charUtf8Len/encodeUtf8 AND the two String-receiver methods decodeUtf8At/utf8CharCount that
// depend on them) lives in kite::core::char. Auto-included via compiler/prelude.conf.
//
// String is a real CLASS holding an OWNED heap byte buffer (no longer a bare NUL-terminated C-string word):
//
//     class String(val n: Int, val data: Int)   // n = byte count (NOT strlen); data = malloc'd bytes
//
// with a `deinit` that frees `data`. Following Kotlin, its convention methods live WITH the type: the
// intrinsic queries/transforms are real MEMBER METHODS inside the `class String { ... }` body —
// `s.charAt(i)`, `s.length()`/`s.length`, `s.byteAt(i)`, `s.toStr()`, `s.substr(...)`, `s.repeat(...)` — while
// the operators are impl blocks: `a + b` -> `impl Add for String`, `a == b` -> `impl Eq for String`,
// `a < b` -> `impl Ord for String`, and `n.toStr()` stays an `Int` extension. Byte access
// is `__rawLoadByte(self.data, i)` (not on the box word), and a result is built into a fresh owned buffer then
// wrapped with `String(count, buf)`. The `@lang` markers record the desugared landing symbols (str.get /
// str.concat / str.show) so the desugar sites resolve exactly the method these operators name.
//
// A string LITERAL is emitted (codegen genStrLit) as a call to __mkStr, which heap-copies the interned bytes
// into an owned buffer so an embedded \x00 is preserved (`"a\x00b".length()` == 3). print/%s and readFile/__argv
// load / box `.data` in codegen (genPrintStrBox / genBoxCstr / genStrPtr).
//
// The count field is named `n` (NOT `length`) so it does not collide with the `String.length()` method (a
// `.length` field + a `length()` getter would cross the getter-routing path). `data` is the 2nd field — codegen hardcodes
// offset 8 for `.data` (genPrintStrBox / genStrPtr), so the field ORDER (n@0, data@8) is load-bearing.

// @arc(manual): the boxed String is ARC-EXEMPT (leak-managed, like the C-string it replaced). WHY, and why
// this is REQUIRED for whole-compiler self-host: the compiler stores String references inside type-erased
// `IntBuf` word slots (symbol tables, interned names, every `List<String>`), which auto-ARC cannot see. With
// auto-ARC active on String, an aliased/moved String whose owning local exits scope is released while a copy
// still lives in a container — and the boxed value's `deinit { __rawFree }` then double-frees the shared byte
// buffer (observed: "malloc: Heap corruption detected, free list is damaged" aborting the compiler
// mid-self-host). Exempting String (no auto retain/release, deinit never auto-runs) makes every string a
// never-freed owned heap blob — memory grows but is never corrupted, exactly the sound behavior the primitive
// String had. The `deinit` body is retained only so a future supervised element-ARC scheme can wire it; it is
// inert while exempt. The @arc(manual) tag on the class decl records "String" as ARC-exempt AND registers it
// as a builtin nominal.
@arc(manual)
class String(val n: Int, val data: Int) {
  deinit { __rawFree(self.data) }

  // Non-allocating queries, now real MEMBER methods (Kotlin-style): they mangle to `String_charAt` /
  // `String_length` / … identically to the former `fun String.charAt(...)` extension form, so this is
  // byte-neutral. `.length()` is the stored byte count (a field load, so an embedded NUL counts); charAt /
  // byteAt load one byte out of the owned buffer. @lang("str.get") makes charAt the landing point for `s[i]`.
  @lang("str.get")
  fun charAt(self, i: Int): Int { return __rawLoadByte(self.data, i) }

  // @lang("str.length"): the for-in bound over a String's bytes resolves here (emitted String_length), the
  // symmetric partner of str.get so `for (c in s)` is single-sourced from HERE, not a hardcoded name.
  // Kotlin names a String's element count `length` (sequences use `size`); a nullary method, so `s.length`
  // (no parens) also resolves here as a getter.
  @lang("str.length")
  fun length(self): Int { return self.n }

  // `s.isEmpty()` — true for the zero-length string. `self.n` is the stored byte count, so `"".isEmpty()`
  // is true and `"a\x00b".isEmpty()` is false (embedded NUL counts, matching length()).
  fun isEmpty(self): Bool { return self.n == 0 }

  // `s.byteAt(i)`: the core-tier byte accessor (§2c — the T0 self-host iteration primitive, distinct from a
  // future `chars()` that decodes UTF-8). Same load as charAt, named per the design.
  fun byteAt(self, i: Int): Int { return __rawLoadByte(self.data, i) }

  // `s.toStr()` on a String is the identity materialize (a String is already owned) — lets a substr/window
  // result be `.toStr()`'d uniformly and is a harmless no-op on a real String.
  fun toStr(self): String { return self }

  // ALLOCATING transforms (moved here from the former kite::alloc::string). These build a FRESH owned String
  // heap buffer. All leak (the boxed String is @arc(manual), never auto-freed) — a substr/repeat result is a
  // fresh owned String box whose deinit is inert while exempt.

  // `s.substr(start, length)`: copy `length` bytes from s+start into a FRESH owned String (a real copy, NOT a
  // view, so callers can `+` it / store it). NUL-terminated.
  fun substr(self, st: Int, len: Int): String {
    val r = __rawAlloc(len + 1)
    var i = 0
    while (i < len) { __rawStoreByte(r, i, __rawLoadByte(self.data, st + i)) i = i + 1 }
    __rawStoreByte(r, len, 0)
    return String(len, r)
  }

  // `s.repeat(n)` — n copies of s, concatenated (n<=0 yields ""). Builds onto ONE StrBuilder (amortized),
  // then seals with finish() — no O(n^2) concat chain, no per-iteration leak.
  fun repeat(self, cnt: Int): String {
    val b = strNew()
    var i = 0
    while (i < cnt) { b.pushStr(self) i = i + 1 }
    return b.finish()
  }
}

// __mkStr(raw, n): box `n` bytes from the raw pointer `raw` into a fresh owned String (NUL-terminated so it
// also round-trips through libc %s). codegen's genStrLit / genBoxCstr emit calls to this for string literals
// and for the raw results of readFile / __argv.
fun __mkStr(raw: Int, n: Int): String {
  val d = __rawAlloc(n + 1)
  var i = 0
  while (i < n) { __rawStoreByte(d, i, __rawLoadByte(raw, i)) i = i + 1 }
  __rawStoreByte(d, n, 0)
  return String(n, d)
}

// The non-allocating queries (charAt / len / length / byteAt) are now real MEMBER methods inside the
// `class String` body above (Kotlin-style), mangling identically to the former `fun String.charAt(...)`
// extension form. The @lang("str.get") role that binds `s[i]` moved with `charAt` onto the class body.

// `n.toStr()`: base-10, handles 0 and negatives, into a fresh owned boxed String. `%` avoided via
// d = mm - (mm/10)*10. (ASCII '0'=48, '-'=45.) @lang("str.show") makes it the interpolation `$expr` show
// landing point for a non-String piece (the "interpolation calls show" path).
@lang("str.show")
fun Int.toStr(): String {
  if (self == 0) { val z = __rawAlloc(2) __rawStoreByte(z, 0, 48) __rawStoreByte(z, 1, 0) return String(1, z) }
  var neg = 0
  var m = self
  if (self < 0) { neg = 1 m = 0 - self }
  var digits = 0
  var t = m
  while (t > 0) { digits = digits + 1 t = t / 10 }
  val total = digits + neg
  val r = __rawAlloc(total + 1)
  __rawStoreByte(r, total, 0)
  var pos = total - 1
  var mm = m
  while (mm > 0) { val d = mm - (mm / 10) * 10 __rawStoreByte(r, pos, 48 + d) pos = pos - 1 mm = mm / 10 }
  if (neg == 1) { __rawStoreByte(r, 0, 45) }
  return String(total, r)
}

// `toStr(n)` — the free-function spelling, delegating to the canonical `Int.toStr` method above.
fun toStr(n: Int): String { return n.toStr() }

// `s.toStr()` on a String (the identity materialize) is now a MEMBER method in the `class String` body above.

// String overloads `+` as concatenation via the Add operator trait (see kite::core::ops): klower's lowerBin
// desugars `a + b` where a (or b) is a String to String_add(a, b). @lang("str.concat") also makes it the
// interpolation-join landing point for a two-piece splice. Allocates n(self)+n(rhs)+1, copies both buffers,
// boxes the result as a FRESH owned String (NUL-terminated). A long chain / a loop should use StrBuilder.
impl Add for String {
  @lang("str.concat")
  fun add(self, rhs: String): String {
    val la = self.n
    val lb = rhs.n
    val d = __rawAlloc(la + lb + 1)
    var i = 0
    while (i < la) { __rawStoreByte(d, i, __rawLoadByte(self.data, i)) i = i + 1 }
    var j = 0
    while (j < lb) { __rawStoreByte(d, la + j, __rawLoadByte(rhs.data, j)) j = j + 1 }
    __rawStoreByte(d, la + lb, 0)
    return String(la + lb, d)
  }
}

// String value-equality via the Eq operator trait (see kite::core::ops): klower's lowerBin desugars
// `a == b`/`a != b` where a (or b) is a String to String_eq(a, b) / not(String_eq(a, b)). Compare the two byte
// windows (length then bytes). Int/Char/other `==` has no Eq impl and stays a machine compare.
impl Eq for String {
  fun eq(self, rhs: String): Bool {
    if (self.n != rhs.n) { return false }
    var i = 0
    while (i < self.n) {
      if (__rawLoadByte(self.data, i) != __rawLoadByte(rhs.data, i)) { return false }
      i = i + 1
    }
    return true
  }
}

// String total order (Ord, kite::core::cmp): lexicographic over UNSIGNED byte values, returning -1/0/1,
// shorter-is-less on a common prefix. klower's ordering-operator desugar (< <= > >=) routes to String_cmp when
// an operand is concretely String (comparing its -1/0/1 result against 0). The compiler's own sources never
// `<`/`>` a String — those stay on the numeric machine-compare path — so this only lights up user-level String
// ordering (and TreeMap keys).
impl Ord for String {
  fun cmp(self, rhs: String): Int {
    var i = 0
    var go = 1
    while (go == 1) {
      if (i >= self.n) { if (i >= rhs.n) { return 0 } return 0 - 1 }
      if (i >= rhs.n) { return 1 }
      val ca = __rawLoadByte(self.data, i)
      val cb = __rawLoadByte(rhs.data, i)
      if (ca < cb) { return 0 - 1 }
      if (ca > cb) { return 1 }
      i = i + 1
    }
    return 0
  }
}

// String hash (Hash, kite::core::cmp): FNV-1a over the byte window, 64-bit machine word (wraps naturally on the
// register). The key contract a hashed RawMap/Set needs for String keys (Eq + Hash). FNV-1a: h =
// 14695981039346656037; for each byte b: h = (h ^ b) * 1099511628211. Both constants are the standard 64-bit
// FNV primes.
impl Hash for String {
  fun hash(self): Int {
    var h = 14695981039346656037
    var i = 0
    while (i < self.n) { h = (h ^ __rawLoadByte(self.data, i)) * 1099511628211 i = i + 1 }
    return h
  }
}

// NOTE on Display/`show`: interpolation's "call show" path is the `str.show` role (Int_toStr for a non-String
// piece; a String piece passes through unchanged). A prelude `trait Display { fun show }` is deliberately NOT
// added here — the dynamic `.show()` dispatch keys on the method NAME, and a user `trait Show { fun show }`
// (see the generic-trait-bound tests) already owns that name; a second prelude trait on the same method would
// cross the two dispatchers. String Display therefore rides the identity `str.show` passthrough, not a
// colliding trait impl.
//
// The growable StrBuilder (byte-buffer) builder + its strNew/strFrom ctors live in their own module,
// kite::core::strbuilder (auto-included right after this one via compiler/prelude.conf).

// --- ALLOCATING transforms ---
// The String-receiver allocating transforms `s.substr(...)` and `s.repeat(...)` are now MEMBER methods in the
// `class String` body above (each builds a FRESH owned String heap buffer; all leak while @arc(manual)).
//
// The string-join helper is NO LONGER a free `joinList(xs: IntBuf, sep)` over the erased IntBuf (which
// blindly treated each Int word as a String pointer — a footgun once `[..]` yields Vec<T>). It is now the
// footgun-free `Vec<T>.join(self, sep)` method in kite::alloc::collections::vec: each element is RENDERED to
// a string via interpolation (the `str.show` role), so in each monomorphized specialization the concrete T's
// renderer resolves — `Vec<Int>.join` prints "1,2,3", `Vec<String>.join` concatenates the strings — with no
// "assume the element is already a String" crash.

// NOTE: the UTF-8 String-receiver methods `s.decodeUtf8At(i)` / `s.utf8CharCount()` stay in kite::core::char
// (the opt-in UTF-8 tier), NOT here: `utf8CharCount` calls the Int-tier `utf8SeqLen`, which is a char.kite
// method and NOT in the prelude, so hoisting these into the always-loaded core String module would make the
// prelude reference an unloaded symbol (breaks self-host). They live WITH `utf8SeqLen` in char.kite instead.
